  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and a few thunderstorms late Saturday\nafternoon into Sunday. Gusty post frontal winds on Sunday. \n\nQuiet weather into most of Saturday. Southwest flow will bring\ntemperatures well above normal Saturday and some record highs\nmay be possible. See climate section below.\n\nA strong front tracks across Saturday night exiting to the east\nSunday morning. Expect a better coverage of showers. Some\ninstability late Saturday afternoon in the mountains for a few\nthunderstorms. \n\nThough coverage of showers should be better not everyone will\nsee rain, and on average most will see less than a tenth of an\ninch, with a quarter inch or so possible in the mountains of WV\nand far SW VA with any storms.\n\nNorthwest winds pick up as the front moves through so should see\ngusts 20 to 35 mph possible Sunday afternoon, while temperatures\nfall into the 30s and 40s in the mountains by mid afternoon.\n\nLarge surface high pressure arrives behind the front to start the \nwork week, until another frontal systems starts to drop southward \ntowards the area during the Wednesday/Thursday timeframe. Rebounding \ntemperatures and increasing moisture will lead to widely\nscattered showers and possible thunderstorms with the front \nnearby and mainly in the mountains, but initial amounts look to\nbe limited at this time. Without much wetting rainfall expected\neast of the Blue Ridge through the next seven days, can \nanticipate drought conditions to remain or worsen for those \nareas, and will likely have to monitor for fire weather concerns\nMonday with gusty northwesterly winds. See the Fire Weather \nSection for more details on fire danger through the week.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Post frontal airmass will bring more seasonal\ntemperatures, with a couple chances for frost/freeze Sunday\nnight and Monday night.\n\nUpper level trough remains in place early next week providing\ncooler temps especially nighttime lows. This trough pushes east\nmidweek with building heights/temps. Cold air advection behind \nthe front Sunday night into Monday morning will allow \ntemperatures to fall into the low to mid 30s for areas west of \nthe Blue Ridge. \n\nSurface high pressure overhead Monday night looks to provide\nmore ideal conditions for frost/freeze especially the mountains.",
